    "secondarySkillIds": [],
    "reasoningScore": 35,
    "difficulty": "Development",
    "difficultyReason": "Distinguishes absolute-value transformations of inputs and outputs, including combined transformations and symmetry."
  },
  "content": {
    "id": "p23-stem-1-8-node",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "1",
    "prompt": {
      "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
      "version": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p23-true-false-instruction",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 2,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "True or False?",
              "marks": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p23-q01",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "The graph of $y=|f(x)|$ is a reflection of the curve across the $x$-axis.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "False. Only the part below the $x$-axis is reflected across the $x$-axis. The part on or above the axis remains unchanged.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q02",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "The graph of $y=-|f(x)|$ reflects the part of the curve above the $x$-axis to below the $x$-axis.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. Where $f(x)>0$, $-|f(x)|=-f(x)$, so the part above the $x$-axis is reflected below it. Where $f(x)\\leq 0$, $-|f(x)|=f(x)$, so that part remains unchanged.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q03",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "The graph of $y=f(|x|)$ only has positive $x$-coordinates because $|x|\\geq 0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "False. The input to $f$ is nonnegative, but $x$ can be negative. Keep the right half of the original graph and reflect a copy across the $y$-axis.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q04",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "The graph of $y=|f(x)|$ can also be expressed as\n$$y=\\begin{cases}f(x)&\\text{if }f(x)\\geq 0,\\\\-f(x)&\\text{if }f(x)<0.\\end{cases}$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. Absolute value preserves a nonnegative output and changes the sign of a negative output.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q05",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "The graph of $y=|f(|x|)|$ only appears in the first quadrant.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "False. The outputs are nonnegative, but $x$ can have either sign. The graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis and may occupy both the first and second quadrants, as well as the axes.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q06",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "The graph of $y=f(-|x|)$ does not use the right-half of the $y=f(x)$ graph.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. Since $-|x|\\leq 0$, only the original graph at nonpositive inputs is used. Keep its left half and reflect a copy across the $y$-axis, retaining the point at $x=0$ when defined.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q07",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "g",
        "prompt": "The graph of $y=|f(|x|)|$ is symmetric in both the $x$ and $y$-axes.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "False. It is symmetric about the $y$-axis because $|x|$ is unchanged when $x$ changes sign. Its outputs are nonnegative, so it is not generally symmetric about the $x$-axis.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q08",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "h",
        "prompt": "If $f$ is odd, the graph of $y=|f(x)|$ is also odd.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "False. Since $f$ is odd,\n$$\\begin{align*}|f(-x)|&=|-f(x)|\\\\|f(-x)|&=|f(x)|.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $y=|f(x)|$ is even. It is also odd only in the special case that it is identically zero.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      }
    ]
  },
